 ('p', "The three temperature scales each reflect the priorities of their creators, which is why they sit at different zeros. Fahrenheit, devised in the early eighteenth century, set its zero at the coldest temperature its inventor could reliably reproduce with a brine mixture, and placed body temperature and water's freezing point at seemingly arbitrary numbers, which is exactly why it feels irregular today. Celsius came later with a cleaner idea: put zero at water's freezing point and one hundred at its boiling point, giving a scale neatly divided into a hundred degrees, ideal for everyday and scientific use alike."),
